ude sensitive information (API keys, tokens) in code or commits\\\" \\n- Avoid listing every component or file structure that can be easily discovered\\n- Don't include generic development practices\\n- If there are Cursor rules (in .cursor/rules/ or .cursorrules) or Copilot rules (in .github/copilot-instructions.md), make sure to include the important parts.\\n- If there is a README.md, make sure to include the important parts. \\n- Do not make up information such as \\\"Common Development Tasks\\\", \\\"Tips for Development\\\", \\\"Support and Documentation\\\" unless this is expressly included in other files that you read.\\n- Be sure to prefix the file with the following text:\\n\\n```\\n# CLAUDE.md\\n\\nThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\\n```\"}]},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01YWkoRsYzU3CYdccosnYobe\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/yunwei37/agent-tracer/CLAUDE.md\"}}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01YWkoRsYzU3CYdccosnYobe\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"     1→# CLAUDE.md\\n     2→\\n     3→This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\\n     4→\\n     5→## Overview\\n     6→\\n     7→AgentSight is a comprehensive observability framework designed specifically for monitoring AI agent behavior through SSL/TLS traffic interception and process monitoring. Unlike traditional application-level instrumentation, AgentSight observes at the system boundary using eBPF technology, providing tamper-resistant insights into AI agent interactions with minimal performance overhead.\\n     8→\\n     9→## Project Structure\\n    10→\\n    11→- **`src/`**: Core eBPF programs and C utilities\\n    12→  - `process.bpf.c` & `process.c`: Process monitoring eBPF program with lifecycle tracking\\n    13→  - `sslsniff.bpf.c` & `sslsniff.c`: SSL/TLS traffic monitoring eBPF program\\n    14→  - `test_process_utils.c`: Unit tests for process utilities\\n    15→  - `Makefile`: Advanced build configuration with AddressSanitizer support\\n    16→- **`collector/`**: Rust-based streaming analysis framework\\n    17→  - `src/framework/`: Core streaming analysis framework with pluggable analyzers\\n    18→    - `analyzers/`: HTTP parsing, chunk merging, file logging, output handling\\n    19→    - `runners/`: SSL, Process, and Fake data runners\\n    20→    - `core/events.rs`: Standardized event system with JSON payloads\\n    21→    - `binary_extractor.rs`: Embedded eBPF binary management\\n    22→  - `src/main.rs`: CLI entry point with multiple operation modes\\n    23→  - `DESIGN.md`: Detailed framework architecture documentation\\n    24→- **`frontend/`**: Next.js web interface for visualization\\n    25→  - React/TypeScript frontend with timeline visualization\\n    26→  - Real-time log parsing and event display\\n    27→- **`script/`**: Python analysis tools\\n    28→  - SSL traffic analyzers and timeline generators\\n    29→  - Data processing and visualization utilities\\n    30→- **`docs/`**: Project documentation\\n    31→  - Problem statement, architectural decisions, and usage guides\\n    32→- **`vmlinux/`**: Kernel headers for different architectures (x86, arm64, riscv)\\n    33→- **`libbpf/`**: libbpf library submodule\\n    34→- **`bpftool/`**: bpftool utility submodule\\n    35→\\n    36→## Common Development Commands\\n    37→\\n    38→### Building the Project\\n    39→\\n    40→```bash\\n    41→# Install dependencies (Ubuntu/Debian)\\n    42→make install\\n    43→\\n    44→# Build eBPF programs\\n    45→make build\\n    46→\\n    47→# Build collector (requires Rust 1.88.0+)\\n    48→cd collector && cargo build --release\\n    49→\\n    50→# Build frontend\\n    51→cd frontend && npm install && npm run build\\n    52→\\n    53→# Run tests\\n    54→cd src && make test\\n    55→\\n    56→# Clean build artifacts\\n    57→make clean\\n    58→cd collector && cargo clean\\n    59→```\\n    60→\\n    61→### Development Commands\\n    62→\\n    63→```bash\\n    64→# Run individual eBPF programs\\n    65→sudo src/process\\n    66→sudo src/sslsniff\\n    67→\\n    68→# Run collector with different modes\\n    69→cd collector && cargo run ssl --sse-merge\\n    70→cd collector && cargo run process\\n    71→cd collector && cargo run agent --comm python --pid 1234\\n    72→\\n    73→# Run frontend development server\\n    74→cd frontend && npm run dev\\n    75→\\n    76→# Run embedded web server for frontend\\n    77→cd collector && cargo run server\\n    78→\\n    79→# Build with AddressSanitizer for debugging\\n    80→cd src && make debug\\n    81→cd src && make sslsniff-debug\\n    82→```\\n    83→\\n    84→### Testing\\n    85→\\n    86→```bash\\n    87→# Run C unit tests\\n    88→cd src && make test\\n    89→\\n    90→# Run Rust tests\\n    91→cd collector && cargo test\\n    92→\\n    93→# Run integration tests with fake data\\n    94→cd collector && cargo test -- --test-threads=1\\n    95→\\n    96→# Frontend linting and type checking\\n    97→cd frontend && npm run lint\\n    98→cd frontend && npm run build  # Also runs type checking\\n    99→```\\n   100→\\n   101→## Architecture Overview\\n   102→\\n   103→### Core Components\\n   104→\\n   105→1. **eBPF Data Collection Layer**\\n   106→   - `process.bpf.c`: Monitors system processes, executions, and file operations\\n   107→   - `sslsniff.bpf.c`: Captures SSL/TLS traffic data with <3% performance overhead\\n   108→   - Both programs output structured JSON events to stdout\\n   109→\\n   110→2. **Rust Streaming Framework** (`collector/src/framework/`)\\n   111→   - **Runners**: Execute eBPF binaries and stream events (SSL, Process, Fake, Agent, Combined)\\n   112→   - **Analyzers**: Process and transform event streams with pluggable architecture\\n   113→   - **Core Events**: Standardized event format with rich metadata and JSON payloads\\n   114→   - **Binary Extractor**: Manages embedded eBPF binaries with automatic cleanup\\n   115→\\n   116→3. **Frontend Visualization** (`frontend/`)\\n   117→   - Next.js/React application for real-time event visualization\\n   118→   - Timeline view with log parsing and semantic event processing\\n   119→   - TypeScript implementation with Tailwind CSS styling\\n   120→\\n   121→4. **Analysis Tools** (`script/`)\\n   122→   - Python utilities for SSL traffic analysis and timeline generation\\n   123→   - Data processing pipelines for correlation analysis\\n   124→\\n   125→### Streaming Pipeline Architecture\\n   126→\\n   127→```\\n   128→eBPF Binary → JSON Output → Runner → Analyzer Chain → Frontend/Storage/Output\\n   129→```\\n   130→\\n   131→### Key Framework Components\\n   132→\\n   133→- **`framework/core/events.rs`**: Core event system with standardized `Event` structure\\n   134→- **`framework/runners/`**: Data collection implementations with fluent builders\\n   135→- **`framework/analyzers/`**: Stream processing plugins (ChunkMerger, FileLogger, Output)\\n   136→- **`framework/binary_extractor.rs`**: Manages embedded eBPF binaries with security\\n   137→\\n   138→### Event Flow\\n   139→\\n   140→1. **Data Collection**: eBPF programs collect kernel events (SSL/TLS, process lifecycle)\\n   141→2. **JSON Streaming**: Events converted to JSON with timestamps and metadata\\n   142→3. **Runner Processing**: Rust runners parse JSON and create typed event streams\\n   143→4. **Analyzer Chain**: Multiple analyzers process events in configurable sequences\\n   144→5. **Output**: Processed events sent to console, files, frontend, or external systems\\n   145→\\n   146→## Development Patterns\\n   147→\\n   148→### Adding New eBPF Programs\\n   149→\\n   150→1. Create `.bpf.c` file with eBPF kernel code using CO-RE (Compile Once - Run Everywhere)\\n   151→2. Create `.c` file with userspace loader and JSON output formatting\\n   152→3. Add to `APPS` variable in `src/Makefile`\\n   153→4. Include appropriate vmlinux.h for target architecture\\n   154→5. Use libbpf for userspace interaction and event handling\\n   155→6. Add unit tests following `test_process_utils.c` pattern\\n   156→\\n   157→### Adding New Analyzers\\n   158→\\n   159→1. Implement the `Analyzer` trait in `collector/src/framework/analyzers/`\\n   160→2. Add async processing logic for event streams using tokio\\n   161→3. Export in `analyzers/mod.rs`\\n   162→4. Use in runner chains via fluent builder pattern `add_analyzer()`\\n   163→5. Follow existing patterns for error handling and stream processing\\n   164→\\n   165→### Adding New Runners\\n   166→\\n   167→1. Implement the `Runner` trait in `collector/src/framework/runners/`\\n   168→2. Use fluent builder pattern for configuration\\n   169→3. Support embedded binary extraction via `BinaryExtractor`\\n   170→4. Add comprehensive error handling and logging\\n   171→5. Export in `runners/mod.rs`\\n   172→\\n   173→### Configuration Management\\n   174→\\n   175→- eBPF programs use command-line arguments for runtime configuration\\n   176→- Collector framework uses fluent builder pattern for type-safe configuration\\n   177→- Binary extraction handled automatically via `BinaryExtractor` with temp file cleanup\\n   178→- Frontend configuration through environment variables and build-time settings\\n   179→\\n   180→## Key Design Principles\\n   181→\\n   182→1. **Streaming Architecture**: Real-time event processing with minimal memory usage and async/await\\n   183→2. **Plugin System**: Extensible analyzer chains for flexible data processing pipelines\\n   184→3. **Error Resilience**: Graceful handling of malformed data, process failures, and analyzer errors\\n   185→4. **Resource Management**: Automatic cleanup of temporary files, processes, and kernel resources\\n   186→5. **Type Safety**: Rust type system ensures memory safety and prevents common vulnerabilities\\n   187→6. **Zero-Instrumentation**: System-level monitoring without modifying target applications\\n   188→\\n   189→## Development Workflow\\n   190→\\n   191→### Typical Development Cycle\\n   192→\\n   193→1. **Build eBPF programs**: `make build` (run from root directory)\\n   194→2. **Build collector**: `cd collector && cargo build`\\n   195→3. **Test changes**: `cd src && make test && cd ../collector && cargo test`\\n   196→4. **Run specific components**: Use individual commands for testing (see Development Commands)\\n   197→5. **Frontend integration**: Use embedded server or Next.js dev server for UI testing\\n   198→\\n   199→### CLI Command Structure\\n   200→\\n   201→The collector uses a subcommand-based CLI:\\n   202→- `ssl`: Monitor SSL/TLS traffic with configurable filtering and analysis\\n   203→- `process`: Monitor process lifecycle events and file operations  \\n   204→- `agent`: Combined monitoring for specific processes (by PID or command name)\\n   205→- `server`: Run embedded web server with frontend for visualization\\n   206→\\n   207→Each subcommand supports its own set of flags and options for customization.\\n   208→\\n   209→## Testing Strategy\\n   210→\\n   211→- **Unit Tests**: C tests for utility functions (`test_process_utils.c`)\\n   212→- **Integration Tests**: Rust tests with `FakeRunner` for full pipeline testing\\n   213→- **Manual Testing**: Direct execution of eBPF programs for validation\\n   214→- **Frontend Testing**: React component and TypeScript type checking\\n   215→- **Performance Testing**: eBPF overhead measurement and memory usage analysis\\n   216→\\n   217→## Security Considerations\\n   218→\\n   219→- eBPF programs require root privileges for kernel access (CAP_BPF, CAP_SYS_ADMIN)\\n   220→- SSL traffic captured includes potentially sensitive data - handle responsibly\\n   221→- Temporary binary extraction requires secure cleanup and proper permissions\\n   222→- Process monitoring may expose system information - use appropriate filtering\\n   223→- Frontend serves processed data - sanitize outputs and validate inputs\\n   224→- Tamper-resistant monitoring design prevents agent manipulation\\n   225→\\n   226→## Dependencies\\n   227→\\n   228→### Core Dependencies\\n   229→- **C/eBPF**: libbpf (v1.0+), libelf, clang (v10+), llvm\\n   230→- **Rust**: tokio (async runtime), serde (JSON), clap (CLI), async-trait, chrono\\n   231→- **Frontend**: Next.js 15.3+, React 18+, TypeScript 5+, Tailwind CSS\\n   232→- **System**: Linux kernel 4.1+ with eBPF support\\n   233→\\n   234→### Development Dependencies\\n   235→- **Rust**: cargo edition 2024, env_logger, tempfile, uuid, hex, chunked_transfer\\n   236→- **Frontend**: ESLint, PostCSS, Autoprefixer, TypeScript compiler\\n   237→- **Python**: Analysis scripts for data processing (optional)\\n   238→- **Web Server**: hyper, hyper-util, rust-embed for embedded frontend serving\\n   239→\\n   240→## Common Issues and Solutions\\n   241→\\n   242→- **Permission Errors**: eBPF programs require sudo privileges - use `sudo` or appropriate capabilities\\n   243→- **Kernel Compatibility**: Use architecture-specific vmlinux.h from `vmlinux/` directory\\n   244→- **Binary Extraction**: Ensure `/tmp` permissions allow execution, check `BinaryExtractor` cleanup\\n   245→- **UTF-8 Handling**: HTTP parser includes safety fixes for malformed data\\n   246→- **Frontend Build**: Ensure Node.js version compatibility and clean `node_modules` if needed\\n   247→- **Cargo Edition**: Project uses Rust edition 2024 - ensure compatible toolchain\\n   248→- **eBPF Program Loading**: If programs fail to load, check for missing BTF support or use fallback vmlinux.h\\n   249→- **Port Conflicts**: Default web server runs on 7395, frontend dev server on 3000\\n   250→\\n   251→## Usage Examples\\n   252→\\n   253→### Basic SSL Traffic Monitoring\\n   254→```bash\\n   255→sudo ./src/sslsniff -p 1234\\n   256→cd collector && cargo run ssl --sse-merge -- -p 1234\\n   257→```\\n   258→\\n   259→### Process Lifecycle Tracking\\n   260→```bash\\n   261→sudo ./src/process -c python\\n   262→cd collector && cargo run process -- -c python\\n   263→```\\n   264→\\n   265→### Combined Agent Monitoring\\n   266→```bash\\n   267→cd collector && cargo run agent --comm python --pid 1234\\n   268→```\\n   269→\\n   270→### Frontend Visualization\\n   271→```bash\\n   272→# Using Next.js development server\\n   273→cd frontend && npm run dev\\n   274→# Open http://localhost:3000/timeline\\n   275→\\n   276→# Using embedded web server in collector\\n   277→cd collector && cargo run server\\n   278→# Open http://localhost:7395/timeline\\n   279→```\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_0139iwuVcbmvVzsxsZrJ5irL\",\"name\":\"Task\",\"input\":{\"description\":\"Analyze codebase architecture\",\"prompt\":\"I need to analyze the codebase to understand the recent changes and improvements to the CLAUDE.md file. Please search for:\\n\\n1. Recent CLI command changes - look for new subcommands, especially \\\"trace\\\" and \\\"record\\\" commands\\n2. Web server integration - look for server functionality and log file serving\\n3. Any new analyzer or runner components\\n4. Frontend API integration patterns\\n5. Any missing important development commands or workflows\\n\\nFocus on finding architectural changes that would help future Claude instances understand the current state of the project better than what's already documented in CLAUDE.md.\"}}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_0139iwuVcbmvVzsxsZrJ5irL\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Based on my analysis of the codebase, I've identifi